From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 04:02:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: sep: j604: read-only until Touch ID is turned on,
 strict xART guard

On the J604 profile:

- xart_writes defaults to off (xart_writes_default = false), and so does
  provision_keybag, which follows it. Loading apple_sep by hand, or by any
  path other than omarchy-mac's enable-fingerprint (which writes
  "options apple_sep xart_writes=1 provision_keybag=1"), writes nothing to
  the shared gigalocker.
- The xART guard is strict (xart_strict = true): Linux never rewrites or
  deletes a session record that it did not create, whatever window,
  first-setup state or one-time allowance applies. If the J604's enclave
  needs such a rewrite to create Linux's keybag or to match (J414s and
  J616s did, J413 did not; J604 is TO-VERIFY), the request fails closed:
  Touch ID is unavailable and the record keeps its value. The two shared
  anti-replay roots stay writable at level 1, since every match updates
  them; that is the remaining shared write.

static_asserts pin both values for J604 and the unchanged defaults for
T8132/J713 and T8122.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig    | 4 ++++
 drivers/soc/apple/profile.rs | 6 ++++++
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
index ba57db91f8..cb7f68b0f7 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
@@ -160,6 +160,10 @@ config APPLE_SEP
 	  layouts fail closed. A Linux identity keybag is created automatically
 	  when none exists; existing identities and reference keys are retained.
 	  Set xart_writes=0 provision_keybag=0 for read-only operation.
+	  On the MacBook Pro 14" M4 (J604) the driver is read-only until
+	  xart_writes=1 is set (omarchy-mac's enable-fingerprint sets it), and
+	  its xART guard is strict: it never rewrites or deletes a session
+	  record that existed before Linux created it.
 	  With writes off the driver still attaches, but refuses Touch ID enrol
 	  and verify: the enclave saves anti-replay state after every match.
 
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/profile.rs b/drivers/soc/apple/profile.rs
index 2f56cdacf6..3211e8de9e 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/profile.rs
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/profile.rs
@@ -484,6 +484,11 @@ pub(crate) const T8132_J604: PlatformProfile = PlatformProfile {
         controller_base: 0x3_ad10_8000,
         ..T8122.sensor
     },
+    // [j604] Linux never changes a session record it did not create, and
+    // writes nothing to the shared gigalocker until the owner turns Touch ID
+    // on. See `xart_guard::strict` and the lab's WRITE-AUDIT.md.
+    xart_strict: true,
+    xart_writes_default: false,
     ..T8122
 };
 
@@ -508,6 +513,7 @@ static_assert!(T8132.nonce_session && T8132.wide_dma_mask);
 static_assert!(T8132_J604.sensor.controller_base == T8132.sensor.controller_base);
 static_assert!(T8132_J604.sensor.expected_id == T8122.sensor.expected_id);
 static_assert!(!T8132_J604.nonce_session && T8132_J604.wide_dma_mask);
+static_assert!(T8132_J604.xart_strict && !T8132_J604.xart_writes_default);
 static_assert!(!T8132.xart_strict && T8132.xart_writes_default);
 static_assert!(!T8122.xart_strict && T8122.xart_writes_default);
 
